(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-ent-pathophysiology-quiz"]={"title":"ENT Pathophysiology Quiz 1 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Middle ear effusion and cholesteatoma impair conduction by which mechanism?","choices":["Obstruction","Mass loading","Stiffness effect","Discontinuity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both add fluid or tissue weight that damps movement of the tympanic membrane and ossicles. The chain is intact and mobile in principle, but it is carrying extra mass. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Above what sound level does long-term chronic exposure cause irreversible cochlear damage, and what is the cellular change?","choices":["Above 85 decibels, by stereocilia degeneration and apoptosis","Above 85 decibels, causing reversible stereocilia fatigue","Above 120 decibels, causing tympanic membrane rupture","Above 60 decibels, causing endolymphatic hydrops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both words matter: degeneration of the stereocilia and programmed death of the hair cells. Neither regenerates, which is why the loss is permanent. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the defect in conductive hearing loss, and where does it sit?","choices":["Destruction of hair cells or auditory nerve fibres, in the inner ear","Failure of glutamate release at cranial nerve VIII","Defective sound transmission, in the external or middle ear","Central processing failure in the auditory cortex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The apparatus that carries sound inward has failed; the cochlea and nerve are intact. That anatomical split is what the tuning fork tests are detecting. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why does asymmetrical vestibular input produce a false sense of motion?","choices":["The cochlea sends conflicting auditory signals","The brain reads the mismatch between the two sides as movement","Cerebral perfusion falls on one side","The otoliths dissolve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Balance is a comparison, so unequal input is indistinguishable from turning. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the pattern of onset in presbycusis?","choices":["Gradual, symmetrical and bilateral","Sudden and unilateral","Episodic and fluctuating","Gradual but strictly unilateral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is sensorineural. Asymmetry would point away from ageing toward a focal lesion. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How do the causes of anterior and posterior epistaxis differ?","choices":["Anterior from local trauma and dry air; posterior from hypertension and anticoagulation","Anterior from hypertension and anticoagulation; posterior from digital trauma","Both are caused predominantly by anticoagulation","Anterior from coagulopathy; posterior from low humidity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anterior bleeds are local and mechanical; posterior bleeds reflect a systemic vascular or clotting problem, which is also why they are more profuse and carry airway risk. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the demographic and inheritance pattern of otosclerosis?","choices":["Commonest in older males, with no hereditary component","Commonest in children, autosomal recessive","Young-to-middle-aged females, accelerated by pregnancy; 50% autosomal dominant","Commonest in young females but entirely sporadic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pregnancy acceleration is the memorable part, and variable penetrance explains why the family history is often incomplete. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Why are nasal polyps described as non-neoplastic?","choices":["They are malignant but slow growing","No new tissue is grown; existing mucosa becomes oedematous","They arise from cartilage rather than epithelium","They regress without treatment in every case"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They are waterlogged mucous membrane rather than a growth. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which nodal findings require immediate assessment to exclude lymphoma?","choices":["Tender mobile nodes during a viral upper respiratory infection","Bilateral small nodes in a child with otitis media","Persistent rubbery or matted nodes in older adults","Any palpable cervical node at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reactive nodes are usually tender and follow a regional infection. Rubbery, matted, persistent and supraclavicular in an older patient is the combination that changes the level of concern. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How are acute and chronic rhinosinusitis defined by duration, and what commonly accompanies the chronic form?","choices":["Acute under 2 weeks; chronic beyond 6 weeks, often with septal deviation","Acute under 4 weeks; chronic beyond 12 weeks despite therapy, often with nasal polyps","Acute under 12 weeks; chronic beyond 6 months, often with turbinate hypertrophy","Acute under 4 weeks; chronic beyond 12 weeks, usually with no mucosal change"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The two thresholds are the definition. Acute disease is usually viral &mdash; rhinovirus or influenza &mdash; with secondary bacterial infection by Streptococcus pneumoniae or Haemophilus influenzae. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What do the semicircular canals detect, and how?","choices":["Linear acceleration and gravitational position, via the otoliths","Rotational acceleration, by endolymph bending the cupula","Sound frequency, via the basilar membrane","Middle ear pressure, via the Eustachian tube"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three loops in the sagittal, coronal and transverse planes. The fluid lags behind the head, the gelatinous cupula bends, and the hair cell stereocilia inside it are stimulated. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the characteristic frequency pattern of presbycusis, and why does it impair conversation?","choices":["Low frequencies go first, taking the vowels","All frequencies fall equally","Only frequencies above the speech range are affected","High frequencies go first, taking the speech consonants"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Consonants such as /s/, /f/ and /t/ are high-frequency sounds and carry most of the discriminating information in speech, which is why voices become hard to make out in a noisy room while still being audible. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which single event in otosclerosis produces the hearing loss?","choices":["Ankylosis of the stapes footplate","Erosion of the incus by the remodelled bone","Hair cell loss at the cochlear base","Accumulation of endolymph in the scala media"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Once the footplate is fixed it cannot transfer mechanical vibration into the cochlear fluid, and conduction fails progressively. Fixation is the whole lesion. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What drives dizziness or lightheadedness, as distinct from vertigo?","choices":["Asymmetrical input between the two vestibular apparatus","Dislodged otoconia in the semicircular canals","Endolymphatic hydrops","Cerebral hypoperfusion or metabolic imbalance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. None of these is vestibular, which is why the sensation is of impending faint rather than of the room moving. Getting this distinction right is the first branch point in the whole workup. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What defines vertigo, and what distinguishes it from dizziness?","choices":["A sensation of impending faint from cerebral hypoperfusion","Any unsteadiness on standing","Loss of consciousness preceded by spinning","A hallucination of motion, with NO syncope"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asymmetry between the two sides is the mechanism, and the absence of syncope is the separator. Dizziness or lightheadedness is a non-vestibular sensation of impending faint. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What makes the middle ear vulnerable to the pressure changes behind otitis media?","choices":["It is fluid-filled and cannot compress","It is an air-filled space vented only by the Eustachian tube","It has no blood supply","It is lined by keratinised skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A sealed air pocket with one vent is why closing the vent drops the pressure and draws in fluid. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which cytokines drive nasal polyp formation, and which cell floods the tissue?","choices":["Interleukins 1, 6 and tumour necrosis factor, with neutrophil influx","Interferon gamma, with lymphocyte influx","Interleukins 4, 5 and 13, with eosinophil influx","Interleukin 17, with mast cell influx"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is type 2 inflammation, and naming the three interleukins is the molecular-mechanism objective in action. Polyps are benign, non-neoplastic oedematous masses from the sinus ostia or ethmoid air cells. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What happens to the wider nasal cavity opposite a septal deviation?","choices":["Compensatory inferior turbinate hypertrophy","It atrophies from disuse","It develops polyps from the increased flow","Nothing changes; only the narrow side is affected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The wide side does more of the work, so its turbinate enlarges to condition the extra airflow &mdash; which can then narrow the only good airway the patient had. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which organism is the usual bacterial cause of tonsillitis, and which finding suggests abscess?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae; bilateral exudate","Pseudomonas aeruginosa; fever above 39 degrees","Haemophilus influenzae; odynophagia","Group A Streptococcus; asymmetric deviation with trismus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Deviation and trismus indicate the infection has collected rather than staying confined to the tonsil and pharyngeal mucosa. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Depolarisation of the cochlear hair cell comes from an ion influx. Through what, and out of which fluid?","choices":["Efflux into the perilymph through voltage-gated channels","Influx from the endolymph through the tip-link channels","Influx from the perilymph across the basilar membrane","Release from intracellular stores after glutamate binding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The endolymph is the reservoir, and opening the tip-link channels lets ions rush in. Depolarisation then opens voltage-gated channels and glutamate is released onto cranial nerve VIII. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which structure separates the external ear from the middle ear, and what does it do?","choices":["The oval window, which passes energy to the cochlea","The round window, which relieves pressure","The Eustachian tube, which equalises pressure","The tympanic membrane, which vibrates and passes energy to the ossicles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the boundary and the first moving part of the conducting chain, which is why perforating it causes conductive loss. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why does the vertigo of Meniere disease come in attacks rather than continuously?","choices":["Pressure builds until micro-ruptures occur, then re-equilibrates","The virus replicates in waves","Otoconia settle between episodes","Inflammation resolves and recurs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cycle of build-up and release is what makes it episodic. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How do cisplatin and carboplatin cause permanent bilateral sensorineural loss?","choices":["They generate reactive oxygen species that destroy outer hair cells at the cochlear base","They inhibit prestin in outer hair cells","They cross-link DNA in stria vascularis cells, compromising endolymph ion homeostasis","They fix the stapes footplate in the oval window"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The stria vascularis maintains the ionic composition of endolymph, and without that gradient transduction fails. Damage there is permanent and bilateral. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which organisms cause 80 to 90 per cent of otitis externa, and what predisposes to the fungal form?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae; fungal disease follows swimming","Moraxella catarrhalis alone; fungal disease follows trauma","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus; fungal after antibiotics","Respiratory syncytial virus and rhinovirus; fungal disease follows steroid use"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The bacterial pair dominate, and Aspergillus niger or Candida albicans take over when the bacterial flora has been suppressed or the canal stays wet. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which set of causes belongs to sensorineural rather than conductive loss?","choices":["Presbycusis, ototoxic drugs, noise trauma and acoustic neuroma","Cerumen impaction, otosclerosis, otitis media and tympanic membrane perforation","Cholesteatoma, exostoses, foreign body and ossicular necrosis","Eustachian tube dysfunction, middle ear effusion and stapes fixation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All four damage the cochlea or the nerve. Note acoustic neuroma sits here because cranial nerve VIII is part of the sensorineural pathway even though the tumour is outside the cochlea. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which two ototoxic effects are described as reversible or symptom-limited rather than permanent?","choices":["Gentamicin and tobramycin damage","Cisplatin and carboplatin damage","Noise trauma and presbycusis","Furosemide on the strial potential; salicylate on prestin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The loop diuretic effect on the strial potential is reversible, and the salicylate effect on the outer hair cell motor protein produces tinnitus. Neither destroys the cells outright. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why does prolonged antibiotic use predispose to FUNGAL otitis externa?","choices":["It thins the canal skin directly","It removes the bacterial competition that normally limits fungal growth","It raises the canal pH to favour fungi","It damages the tympanic membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The same logic as thrush: clear the residents and the fungus has the canal to itself. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which way does the nystagmus of labyrinthitis beat?","choices":["Horizontal-rotary, with the fast phase beating TOWARD the affected side","Vertical and non-suppressible","Absent, because hearing rather than balance is affected","Horizontal-rotary, with the fast phase beating AWAY from the affected side"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The damaged side is under-firing, so the slow drift is toward it and the corrective fast phase beats away. Severe postural instability, gait ataxia and nausea come with it. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of Meniere disease?","choices":["Defective endolymph resorption causing hydrops, ballooning the scala media until micro-ruptures occur","Inflammatory swelling and vascular congestion of the labyrinth after a viral infection","Dislodged otoconia floating in the semicircular canals","Demyelination of the vestibular nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Endolymphatic hydrops is the lesion: fluid accumulates because it is not resorbed, the membranous labyrinth distends, and micro-ruptures follow. The episodic nature follows from that cycle. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why does anticoagulation favour POSTERIOR rather than anterior epistaxis?","choices":["The anterior septum has no blood supply","Anticoagulants are concentrated posteriorly","Posterior bleeding is driven by systemic vascular and clotting factors","The posterior mucosa is thinner"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anterior causes are local insults to the mucosa; posterior causes act on the vessels and on clotting. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"}]};
